    {{xml .Line.Username}}
    {{xml .Line.AuthName}}
    {{xml .Line.Password}}
    {{xml .Line.DisplayName}}
{{- /* DNS mode and backup server (phone-feature-parity S-20), account 1, keys per Grandstream's own configuration templates (config-template-10212026.zip; 2026-11-09 review). P103 = DNS Mode: 0 A Record, 1 SRV, 2 NAPTR/SRV (3 Use Configured IP takes IPv4 addresses of at most 15 characters in P2308 to P2310, no port, so it cannot carry a backup) in wp8x6_config_1.0.3.39, wp810_822_825_config_1.0.11.83, wp820_config_1.0.7.90 and wp856_config_1.0.3.16. Written always, so a phone taken back out of SRV mode returns to A records. In SRV mode (Server.DNSMode "srv", HELLO_PROV_SIP_SRV) P47 is the name whose _sip._udp SRV records list the servers (no port); P29095 = 1 (Register Before DNS SRV Fail-over, 0 No, 1 Yes) registers with the next server before failing calls over to it; P26040 = 3 (DNS SRV Failover Mode: 0 Default, 1 saved one until DNS TTL, 2 saved one until no response, 3 failback follows the failback expiration timer, in all four templates) makes the phone return to the first server when the timer ends; P60056 = 60 is that Failback Timer in minutes (wp8x6, wp820 and wp856 templates; the WP810/822/825 template lists the Failback Timer under P60055, the retry count's own key, which is a mistake in that template, so P60056 is not written to it and it keeps its default, 60). Otherwise, with a backup server (HELLO_PROV_SIP_BACKUP_SERVERS) three keys name it, each host:port: P2312 = Secondary SIP Server (WP8x6, WP810/822/825 and WP856 templates; the WP820 has none), and P48 = Outbound Proxy repeating the primary with P2333 = Backup Outbound Proxy ("Secondary Outbound Proxy" in the WP8x6, WP820 and WP856 templates), which every template has; the phone uses the backup when the primary fails. With none, P48, P2312 and P2333 are left as they are. Not confirmed on a phone (verify, lab item "failover"). */}}
{{- if eq .Server.DNSMode "srv"}}
    1
    1
    3
{{- if ne .Phone.WP "WP8xx"}}
    60
{{- end}}
{{- else}}
    0
{{- with .Server.Backup}}
{{- if or (eq $.Phone.WP "WP8x6") (eq $.Phone.WP "WP8xx") (eq $.Phone.WP "WP856") (eq $.Phone.WP "")}}
    {{xml .Host}}:{{.Port}}
{{- end}}
    {{xml $.Server.Host}}:{{$.Server.Port}}
    {{xml .Host}}:{{.Port}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
{{- /* SIP transport (phone-feature-parity S-13, S-14): P130 = SIP Transport, 0 UDP, 1 TCP, 2 TLS/TCP, account 1, same key and values in Grandstream's wp8x6_config_1.0.3.39, wp810_822_825_config_1.0.11.83, wp820_config_1.0.7.90, wp856_config_1.0.3.16 and ht813_config_1.0.19.6. Server.Transport is "tcp" for a model HELLO_PROV_SIP_TCP_MODELS lists (P130 = 1, no TLS-only keys; S-13). P2331 (Use Actual Ephemeral Port in Contact with TCP/TLS, default 0) and P60065 (Enable TCP Keep-alive, default 1) are in the same templates and stay at their defaults: the Contact keeps the listening port and Kamailio reuses the connection (phones_tcp alias). Server.Transport is "tls" only for a model HELLO_PROV_SIP_TLS_MODELS lists while HELLO_PROV_SIP_TLS_SERVER is set, so a model that fails its TLS lab run is switched back by removing it there. With TLS: P2329 = 1 writes the sips scheme (SIP URI Scheme when using TLS, 0 sip, 1 sips; in all five templates, default 1 in the WP8x6 and WP810/822/825 and 0 in the WP820 and WP856, hence written), and P22293/P22294 are the Minimum and Maximum TLS Version (10 to 13 = TLS 1.0 to 1.3, 99 = no upper limit; written as 12 and 99; the wp8x6_config_1.0.3.39 template lists P22293 and P22294 a second time as the DTLS versions, a duplicate in Grandstream's template). Not confirmed on a phone (verify, lab item 10). The Hello CA the server certificate chains to is installed through P8433 (CA URL above). */}}
{{- if eq .Server.Transport "tls"}}
    2
    1
    12
    99
{{- else if eq .Server.Transport "tcp"}}
    1
{{- else}}
    0
{{- end}}
{{- /* HD voice: Preferred Vocoder choices 1 to 6 of account 1 (P57, P58, P59, P60, P61, P62; Grandstream config templates, "Preferred Vocoder"): 0 PCMU, 8 PCMA, 9 G.722, 123 Opus (the models that have it; payload type P2385 stays at its default 123). Order Opus (the models that have it), G.722, PCMU, PCMA; the choices after the last codec repeat it, which the phone ignores, so no other codec is offered. Another account of a multi-account model has its own choice keys (account 2 P451 to P456, account 3 P551 to P556, and so on in steps of 100); Hello provisions account 1. Unverified on hardware (verify). */}}
{{- if .Codecs.Opus}}
    123
    9
    0
    8
    8
    8
{{- else}}
    9
    0
    8
    8
    8
    8
{{- end}}
    {{div .Server.Expiry 60}}
{{- /* QoS marking (phone-feature-parity S-21): P1558 = Layer 3 QoS for SIP (DSCP), P1559 = Layer 3 QoS for audio (DSCP) [R8x6 TPL]; the same values hello-sip marks its own sockets with (HELLO_DSCP_SIP 26, HELLO_DSCP_RTP 46). UNVERIFIED on hardware. */}}
    {{.Prov.DSCPSIP}}
    {{.Prov.DSCPRTP}}
{{- /* RTCP-XR voice-quality reports (phone-feature-parity S-36), only when HELLO_PROV_RTCPXR is on (off, the phone keeps its default, an empty collector = no reports). Keys, account 1, from Grandstream's own configuration templates: P26093 VQ RTCP-XR Collector Name, P26094 Collector Address, P26095 Collector Port (default 5060) in wp8x6_config_1.0.3.39, wp810_822_825_config_1.0.11.83 and wp856_config_1.0.3.16; P60087 Collector Address Selection (0 Manual, 1 Auto; Auto uses the registered SIP server when no address is set) in wp8x6_config_1.0.3.39 and wp810_822_825_config_1.0.11.83 only; P8489 VQ RTCP-XR Session Report (0 Disabled, 1 Enabled, default 0; the call-end report Hello stores) in the same two. The WP856 template has no Address Selection and no Session Report key, so the WP856 gets the collector address spelled out (the SIP server host) and no P8489; the WP820 template has no RTCP-XR key at all and gets none. P8490 Interval Report stays at its default (off): the collector keeps the call-end report. Account 2 is P26193, P60187, P26194, P26195 (same templates). The phone's PUBLISH goes to the SIP server, which is Hello's collector. Behaviour not yet seen on a phone (verify: that the phone reports with Auto and an empty address, and that the WP856 reports with the address set). */}}
{{- if and .Prov.RTCPXR (or (eq .Phone.WP "WP8x6") (eq .Phone.WP "WP8xx") (eq .Phone.WP "WP856"))}}
    {{xml .Server.Host}}
{{- if eq .Phone.WP "WP856"}}
    {{xml .Server.Host}}
{{- else}}
    1

    1
{{- end}}
    {{.Server.Port}}
{{- end}}
{{- /* Presence (phone-feature-parity S-35): the WP phones gain nothing from PUBLISH for Presence or from SUBSCRIBE for Registration [R8x6 section 4], so both stay off and are written so that a phone someone switched on goes back. P188 = PUBLISH for Presence (0 Disabled, 1 Enabled; account 2 is P488) and P2319 = SUBSCRIBE for Registration (0 No, 1 Yes, the reg event of RFC 3680; account 2 is P2419), account 1, in Grandstream's wp8x6_config_1.0.3.39 and wp810_822_825_config_1.0.11.83. The WP820 and WP856 templates have neither key. No WP template has an Eventlist BLF URI or a Presence Eventlist URI key (those exist only in the GRP and GXP templates: P134, P26084 and P2389 "Auto Provision Eventlists" in grp26xx_config_1.0.15.23), so Line.BLFListURI is not written to a WP. */}}
{{- if or (eq .Phone.WP "WP8x6") (eq .Phone.WP "WP8xx")}}
    0
    0
{{- end}}
{{- /* MWI (spec S-1): P99 = Subscribe for MWI [R8x6 TPL, R8x6 section 5; WP822 same key]; P33 = voicemail access number, defaulting to Hello's voicemail feature code. */}}
    1
    {{xml (or .Line.VoicemailCode "*97")}}
{{- /* Keep-alive on Wi-Fi sleep (phone-feature-parity S-9, S-3): a handset that sleeps loses its NAT mapping and its registration unless the phone pings. WP8x6 P2397 = 1 enables the SIP OPTIONS keep-alive (R8x6 TPL, section 2); the interval (30 s) and max-tries keys are not in the vendor template and stay at their defaults until the lab confirms them (verify). The WP822 has the RFC 5626 CRLF keep-alive as P84989; it is written 0 (off) because the OPTIONS keep-alive and the registration refresh already hold the flow, and Hello answers a double CRLF only over TCP and TLS (R822 section 2; verify the key on a phone's exported XML). */}}
{{- if eq .Phone.Family "8x6"}}
    1
{{- else if eq .Phone.Family "8xx"}}
    0
{{- end}}
{{- /* Text messages (SIP MESSAGE, RFC 3428): P22650 = 1 makes account 1 the designated account for instant messaging (WP8x6 Administration Guide "Instant Message" and the phone-feature-parity spec S-8, R8x6 section 5). Hello answers 202/200 and delivers to every phone of the extension. UNVERIFIED on hardware: not confirmed on a WP826 or WP822, and the value (1 = account 1) is read from the guide, not from a phone's exported configuration. Phones without the key ignore it. */}}
    1
{{- /* Send Anonymous (account 1: P65; accounts 2 and 3 are P421 and P521 in wp8x6_config_1.0.3.39, wp810_822_825_config_1.0.11.83, wp820_config_1.0.7.90 and wp856_config_1.0.3.16: "Send Anonymous (caller ID will be blocked if set to Yes). 0 - No, 1 - Yes. Default is 0"). Written as 1 only when the operator turned on the extension's "Phone sends anonymous" setting; otherwise nothing is written and the phone keeps its own value. The phone then blanks its caller ID on every call, internal ones included, unlike the extension's trunk-only anonymous outbound flag. */}}
{{- if .Line.SendAnonymous}}
    1
{{- end}}
{{- /* Call waiting (phone-feature-parity S-18), written from the extension's flag so the phone agrees with Hello. P26063 = account 1 "Enable Call Waiting" (WP8x6) / "Disable Call Waiting" (WP822): 1 = on, 2 = off in both templates (wp8x6_config_1.0.3.39 reads 0 Default, 1 Yes, 2 No; wp810_822_825_config_1.0.11.83 reads 0 Default, 1 No, 2 Yes, the question inverted). P91 = the phone-wide switch: 0 = on, 1 = off in both. P186 = "Enable Call Waiting Tone" (WP8x6 and WP820 templates): 0 = tone, 1 = no tone; the WP822 template has no such key and ignores it, its tone is P347/P1555 which stay at the phone default. Taken from Grandstream's published config templates, not yet confirmed on a phone (verify, lab item 26). */}}
{{- /* The WP856 template (wp856_config_1.0.3.16: "Enable Call Waiting. 0 Default, 1 No, 2 Yes") reads P26063 the other way round: 2 = on, 1 = off, and has no P91 or P186. */}}
{{- if .Phone.CWInverted}}
    {{if .Line.CallWaiting}}2{{else}}1{{end}}
{{- else if .Line.CallWaiting}}
    1
    0
    0
{{- else}}
    2
    1
    1
{{- end}}
{{- /* Feature Key Synchronization (spec S-7): the phone's DND and call-forward keys follow Hello and Hello's follow the phone, over SUBSCRIBE/NOTIFY Event: as-feature-event. P2325 = Feature Key Synchronization, account 1 (account 2 is P2425, 3 is P2525); P198 = Special Feature, 102 = Broadsoft, which the Administration Guide says the feature needs ("When using this feature, Special Feature needs to be set to BroadSoft"). Both keys are in Grandstream's own config templates (config-template-10212026.zip) for the GXP11xx/14xx/16xx/21xx/22xx, GRP26xx, WP810/820/822/825/856/8x6 and DP75x with the same numbers on every model; the WP820 template documents P2325 as "0 Disable, 1 BroadSoft/Metaswitch". Verified against the vendor templates and guides, not on a phone (verify). */}}
    102
    1
{{- /* Ring map (phone-feature-parity S-44). WP8x6: P95026 maps Bellcore-dr1 to dr5 to a ringtone each (WP8x6 release notes, firmware 1.0.1.85 and later; Hello sends Bellcore-drN in the Alert-Info); written as five comma-separated tones. WP810/820/822/825: the "Match Incoming Caller ID" rules from P1488 with the Alert-Info match type (WP8xx release notes), written as the match text at P1488 + 2 (n - 1) and its ringtone in the next P-value, ten rules; P104 is the account ringtone, Hello's internal tone. Tones: 0 system ring tone, 1 to 3 custom ring tones. The WP856 and GRP/GXP desk phones have no known key and get none. Layouts unverified on a phone (verify). */}}
{{- if and (eq .Phone.Family "8x6") .Ring.Bellcore}}
    {{.Ring.Bellcore}}
{{- end}}
{{- if eq .Phone.Family "8xx"}}
{{- range .Ring.Rules}}

{{xml .Text}}

{{.Tone}}
{{- end}}
{{- with .Ring.Default}}
    {{.}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
{{- /* Local call features (spec S-7): P191 = Enable (Local) Call Features, default 1 on the WP8x6 and WP810/822/825 and 0 on the WP856. On, the phone acts on *72/*73/*90-*93 and its own DND and forward menus itself and Hello never sees them. Off, the phone dials the codes to Hello, whose feature codes are written below as the strings the DND and forward keys dial. Switching it off also hands the phone's other star codes (caller ID *67/*82, call waiting *70/*71) to Hello, which does not implement them. Keys per Grandstream's templates: WP8x6 (wp8x6_config_1.0.3.39) P2344/P2345 DND on/off, P26013/P26014 forward always on/off, P26009/P26010 busy, P26011/P26012 no answer (the _target fields P29092-P29094 stay empty so the user's key asks for the number); WP856 (wp856_config_1.0.3.16) only P2344/P2345; WP810/822/825 (wp810_822_825_config_1.0.11.83) only P191. Record key: the WP8x6 P26028/P26029 are the codes the Record key sends to turn recording on and off (P29066 = 0 key default: on), set to Hello's *1 toggle; the WP856 P6760 = 2 Record Mode "on UCM" hands recording to the server. Verified in the vendor templates, not on a phone (verify). */}}
    0
{{- with .Codes.DNDOn}}
{{- if or (eq $.Phone.Family "8x6") (eq $.Phone.Family "856")}}
    {{xml .}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
{{- with .Codes.DNDOff}}
{{- if or (eq $.Phone.Family "8x6") (eq $.Phone.Family "856")}}
    {{xml .}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
{{- if eq .Phone.Family "8x6"}}
{{- with .Codes.ForwardOn}}
    {{xml .}}
{{- end}}
{{- with .Codes.ForwardOff}}
    {{xml .}}
{{- end}}
{{- with .Codes.ForwardBusyOn}}
    {{xml .}}
{{- end}}
{{- with .Codes.ForwardBusyOff}}
    {{xml .}}
{{- end}}
{{- with .Codes.ForwardNoAnswerOn}}
    {{xml .}}
{{- end}}
{{- with .Codes.ForwardNoAnswerOff}}
    {{xml .}}
{{- end}}
    0
    *1
    *1
{{- end}}
{{- if eq .Phone.Family "856"}}
    2
{{- end}}
{{- /* Syslog (phone-feature-parity S-27): P207 = Syslog Server, P208 = Syslog Level (0 None, 1 Debug, 2 Info, 3 Warning, 4 Error) [R8x6 TPL]; the same keys on the other Grandstream families. Off by default and written explicitly off so turning a group's syslog off reaches phones that had it on. The server is Hello's sink, UDP; a host:port is written as configured (UNVERIFIED on hardware, verify). */}}
{{- if .Syslog}}
    {{xml .Syslog.Address}}
    {{.Syslog.Level}}
{{- else}}

    0
{{- end}}
{{- /* Emergency numbers from Hello's emergency routes (CALL-47): P8385 on the WP8x6, P25675 on the WP856; the WP8xx template has none. Call park and retrieve codes (CALL-16): P60069 and P2336 on the WP8x6 and WP856. */}}
{{- if .Emergency}}
{{- if eq .Phone.Family "8x6"}}
    {{xml .Emergency}}
{{- else if eq .Phone.Family "856"}}
    {{xml .Emergency}}
{{- end}}
{{- end}}
{{- if or (eq .Phone.Family "8x6") (eq .Phone.Family "856")}}
{{- if .Line.ParkCode}}
